heck: earlier 0x07DB=3 (was 2). Maybe 0x07D7-0x07DC is the \"displayed score\" shadow, and 0x07DE-0x07E3 is the actual score. When we wrote 0x07DE-0x07E3, the actual score changed but the shadow didn't, so the HUD (drawn from shadow?) stayed.\n\nWait, but the HUD did show 001430 after the coin — the shadow would have been updated to 001434 too if the game updates it from the score. Unless the update only copies changed digits.\n\nHmm, actually I bet the real situation is: the score RAM is 0x07DE-0x07E3, and the HUD is drawn by a routine that, each time the score changes, redraws ONLY the digits that changed relative to 0x07D7-0x07DC (a shadow copy). We wrote the score but not the shadow; the game compared new score (001434) vs shadow (000000) — that would be ALL digits differ, so it should redraw all → showing 001434. But it showed 001430.\n\nAlternatively the shadow isn't in RAM.\n\nHmm wait, maybe I misread the screenshot. Let me look really carefully at \"001430\". The score text: \"001430\". Hmm. Could the last digit be a \"4\"? A slashed zero looks like \"0\" with a diagonal. A \"4\" in this font... Let me compare with the coin count \"08\" and TIME \"301\". The last char of score in the image: it looks like a 0 with a slash (the \"0\" glyph). Actually at 8px it's hard.\n\nLet me zoom by cropping? I can't easily. But get_pixel_color won't help.\n\nAlternative: trigger another score change of +1 (e.g., not possible easily) or collect another coin (+200 again → hundreds digit 4→6; HUD would show 001630 or 001634).\n\nHmm. Actually, let me reconsider — maybe the display lags one \"event\" behind. In the previous proof attempt (A3), after stomping and waiting, HUD showed 000300 and RAM showed [0,0,0,3,0,0]. That matched exactly! There, the game changed the hundreds digit 2→3 and redrew. And we hadn't written anything then. So the HUD follows the score RAM for the digits the game changes.\n\nSo our write of ones digit 4 wasn't drawn because the game's displayed scor