{"type":"text","text":"Mapped all three HUD values blind (differential search → write-proof → screenshot), and promoted them to the shared mapping file.\n\n## Results\n\n| HUD value | Address | Encoding |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Score** | `$07DE` (6 bytes, `$07DE`–`$07E3`) | BCD, one decimal digit per byte, most-significant first (`$07DE` = hundred-thousands … `$07E3` = ones) |\n| **Coin count** | `$07ED` (2 bytes, `$07ED`–`$07EE`) | BCD, `$07ED` = tens, `$07EE` = ones (00–99) |\n| **TIME** | `$07F8` (3 bytes, `$07F8`–`$07FA`) | BCD digits, `$07F8` = hundreds, `$07F9` = tens, `$07FA` = ones; ticks ~1 unit / 24 frames |\n\n## How each was found and proven\n\n- **Timer** — With Mario idle I waited 120 frames and diffed `decreased` (32 survivors); `$07F8`/`$07FA` were among them. A paused read showed `$07F8`–`$07FA` = `2,5,7` while the HUD read **TIME 257**. Proof by write: wrote `[3,9,9]` (399) → after 30 frames the HUD read **TIME 398** with RAM `[3,9,8]`, and after a further 180-frame run the HUD read **TIME 390** with RAM `[3,9,0]`.\n- **Coins** — A coin grab (hold Right 150 frames, then A 30, into the first ? block) gave one coin and 200 points; diffing `increased` left 283 addresses. Proof by write: wrote `$07ED`/`$07EE` = `[5,5]` (55), collected a coin → HUD redrew to **×56**, RAM `[5,6]`; a second run wrote `[0,7]` → HUD **×08**, RAM `[0,8]`.\n- **Score** — The same 283-survivor diff, narrowed with a Goomba stomp (+100, 36 survivors), showed `$07E1` going `2→3` when the HUD went 000200→000300. Proof by write: with the HUD at 000000 I wrote `$07DE`–`$07E3` = `[0,0,1,2,3,0]` (001230), then collected a coin (+200): RAM became `001430` and the screenshot/nametable HUD read **MARIO 001430**.\n\n**One clean proof frame** covered all three at once: after writing `001230`, coins `07`, timer `399`, a single coin grab produced HUD **MARIO 001430 / ×08 / TIME 390**, matching RAM `$07DE`–`$07E3`=`001430`, `$07ED`/`$07EE`=`08`, `$07F8`–`$07FA`=`390`.\n\n## Caveat worth knowing\n\nThe score's HUD redraw only repaints the digits a point award actually touches, so a hand-written nonzero ones digit (`$07E3`) doesn't appear even after GAME OVER (RAM 001234 still displayed 001230). Since SMB awards are all multiples of 100, that digit is 0 in normal play; the address block still carries the full 6-digit score the game's own arithmetic uses (`000009` + 200 points → `000209` in RAM)."}
